#: ../../../contributing.rst:20
msgid "Changelog entries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:22
msgid ""
"`towncrier <https://pypi.org/project/towncrier/>`_ builds the changelog from "
"news fragments in ``changelog.d/``. Name a fragment ``+topic.type.rst`` "
"before opening a pull request, for example ``+reset-eof.bugfix.rst``. "
"Existing numbered names such as ``1234.bugfix.rst`` remain valid. The "
"release workflow gets the pull request number, the commit author, and its co-"
"authors from the squash commit and links them when it assembles the "
"changelog."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:27
msgid ""
"Use ``feature`` for new capabilities and Python support additions or "
"removals; either causes a minor version bump. Use ``change`` for user-"
"visible behavior changes, ``bugfix`` for corrections, ``doc`` for "
"documentation, ``security`` for security fixes, and ``performance`` for "
"speed or resource improvements. ``deprecation`` announces a future removal; "
"``removal`` describes a removed command or option; ``packaging`` covers user-"
"visible distribution or installation changes. Use ``breaking`` only for an "
"incompatible behavior change that is not a Python support change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:33
msgid ""
"One pull request may carry several fragments, including two of the same "
"type: ``+reset-eof.bugfix.rst`` and ``+reset-output.bugfix.rst`` are "
"separate entries. Write each one as a concrete sentence about the effect for "
"a pipx user. Internal tests, dependency updates, and CI work need no entry; "
"apply ``skip news`` instead. Bot pull requests without a fragment receive "
"that label automatically. If a bot supplies a fragment, the changelog check "
"validates it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:38
msgid ""
"Do not put a pull request link or author credit in the fragment; hand-"
"written credits fail the changelog check. The release workflow appends the "
"squash pull request link and credits the commit's human author and co-"
"authors in the RST changelog. GitHub Release notes use their handles. Keep "
"each fragment wrapped to 120 columns; `prek <https://github.com/j178/prek>`_ "
"formats the generated changelog before the release commit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:45
msgid "Concepts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:47
msgid ""
"pipx manages one **environment** per installed application: a virtual "
"environment under ``PIPX_HOME`` named after its **main package**, plus an "
"optional suffix. ``pipx inject`` adds **injected packages** to an existing "
"environment. pipx resolves ``PIPX_HOME`` and every other location from "
"environment variables, platform defaults, and legacy fallback paths "
"(see :doc:`reference/environment-variables`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:52
msgid ""
"A **backend** installs packages into environments. The pip backend links "
"every environment to one **shared pip** environment through a ``.pth`` file; "
"the uv backend calls ``uv venv`` and ``uv pip`` and needs no shared "
"environment. Each environment runs on an **interpreter** that pipx resolves "
"from ``--python``. When ``--fetch-python`` is ``missing`` or ``always``, "
"pipx downloads a standalone build from `python-build-standalone <https://"
"github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone/releases>`_ and caches it; "
"``pipx interpreter list``, ``prune``, and ``upgrade`` manage those builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:59
msgid ""
"After an install, pipx **exposes** the package's resources: its console and "
"GUI scripts (**apps**), man pages, and shell completions, linked into "
"``PIPX_BIN_DIR``, ``PIPX_MAN_DIR``, and ``PIPX_COMPLETION_DIR``. Each "
"environment's **metadata** records the install source, backend, pip "
"arguments, interpreter, and exposed resources, so ``upgrade``, "
"``reinstall``, and ``install-all`` can rebuild the same state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:64
msgid ""
"``pipx run`` uses the **run cache** instead: temporary environments keyed on "
"the requirements and installer settings, which expire after 14 days. A "
"**script** with :pep:`723` inline metadata works as a package: ``pipx run`` "
"installs its declared dependencies into a cached temporary environment, and "
"``pipx install`` hands either backend the same wheel built from the script "
"and records its path or URL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.rst:69
msgid ""
"**Example: installing black.** ``pipx install black`` resolves the default "
"interpreter, creates the ``black`` environment, and has the backend install "
"``black`` into it. pipx then exposes the ``black`` and ``blackd`` apps and "
"records the source ``black`` and its pip arguments in the metadata. A later "
"``pipx upgrade black`` reads that metadata, upgrades the package with the "
"same backend and arguments, exposes any apps the new version adds, and "
"removes the links of any it dropped."
msgstr ""

